// Grade 3 passages (validated by live-patches/grade-journey/validate.mjs). Built by validate.mjs --write.
export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g3-u1-p01","unit":"G3-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Sea Otters","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Sea otters are some of the most clever animals in the ocean. They live along the cold coasts of the North Pacific Ocean. These furry mammals have many smart ways to stay safe, warm and fed.\n\nFirst, sea otters have amazing fur. Their fur is thicker than the fur of any other animal on Earth. It traps tiny bubbles of air next to their skin. The air keeps the cold water away, so the otters stay warm. Otters spend hours each day cleaning and fluffing their fur to keep it working well.\n\nSea otters are also good at finding food. They dive to the sea floor to catch crabs, clams and sea urchins. Some otters bring up a flat rock, too. Floating on its back, an otter sets the rock on its belly. Then it bangs a clam against the rock until the shell cracks open. Very few animals use tools like this!\n\nFinally, sea otters have a smart way to sleep. They rest while floating on their backs in the water. To keep from drifting apart, they sometimes hold paws with each other. Others wrap themselves in long, floating seaweed called kelp. The kelp helps keep them in one place.\n\nWarm fur, clever tools and safe sleeping help sea otters live in a hard place. These skills make the cold ocean a good home for them.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this passage mostly about?","options":["how sea otters live well in the ocean","how sea otters find homes on land","why sea otters are afraid of water"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What do some otters use to crack open clams?","options":["a sharp shell","a flat rock","their back teeth"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Some otters bring up a flat rock, too."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that otter fur keeps them warm?","options":["They dive to the sea floor to catch crabs, clams and sea urchins.","They rest while floating on their backs in the water.","The air keeps the cold water away, so the otters stay warm."],"answer":2,"evidence":"The air keeps the cold water away, so the otters stay warm."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this passage, what is kelp?","options":["a long seaweed","a flat rock","a kind of crab"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Others wrap themselves in long, floating seaweed called kelp."}],"grade":3,"words":227,"auto":{"fk":3.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u1-p02","unit":"G3-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Seeds on the Move","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Plants cannot walk, but their seeds can still travel a long way. A seed needs space, sunlight and water to grow. If every seed landed right under its parent plant, the young plants would be crowded. So plants have many clever ways to send their seeds to new places.\n\nSome seeds ride on the wind. A dandelion seed has a tiny fluffy top that works like a parachute. A breeze can carry it across a whole field. Maple seeds have flat wings. They spin like little helicopters as they fall.\n\nOther seeds hitch a ride on animals. Burdock seeds are covered in tiny hooks. They stick to the fur of a passing dog or deer. Later, the seeds fall off far away. In fact, these sticky burrs gave an inventor the idea for the hook-and-loop strips on some shoes and jackets.\n\nAnimals help in another way, too. Birds eat berries and fly off. The seeds inside pass through their bodies and drop to the ground in a new spot.\n\nWater can move seeds as well. A coconut is a large seed that floats. It can drift across the sea for weeks and then sprout on a faraway beach.\n\nWind, animals and water all help seeds find new homes. That is how plants spread across the land.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of this passage?","options":["Seeds travel to new places in many ways.","Dandelions are the best plants to grow.","Birds like to eat berries in the fall."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What helps a dandelion seed ride the wind?","options":["flat wings","a fluffy top","tiny hooks"],"answer":1,"evidence":"A dandelion seed has a tiny fluffy top that works like a parachute."},{"type":"why","q":"Why do plants send their seeds far away?","options":["so animals will stop eating them","so the seeds can stay warm","so young plants have room to grow"],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that animals carry seeds?","options":["They stick to the fur of a passing dog or deer.","If every seed landed right under its parent plant, the young plants would be crowded.","It can drift across the sea for weeks and then sprout on a faraway beach."],"answer":0,"evidence":"They stick to the fur of a passing dog or deer."}],"grade":3,"words":218,"auto":{"fk":3.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u1-c01","unit":"G3-U1","pool":"check","title":"Busy Beavers","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Beavers are famous builders. With their strong teeth and clever skills, they change the land and water around them.\n\nA beaver's front teeth are bright orange. They contain iron, which makes them hard and strong. The teeth never stop growing, but chewing on wood keeps them from getting too long. A beaver can chew through a small tree in just a few minutes.\n\nBeavers use the trees they cut to build dams across streams. They pile up sticks, rocks and mud. Slowly, the dam blocks the water, and a deep pond forms behind it. The pond keeps the beavers safe. Their enemies, such as wolves and bears, cannot easily reach them in deep water.\n\nIn the middle of the pond, beavers build a home called a lodge. It looks like a big pile of sticks. The door is hidden underwater, so only a swimmer can get inside. Inside the lodge is a dry room where the family sleeps and raises its babies, called kits.\n\nBeaver ponds help other animals, too. Ducks, frogs, fish and turtles all find homes in the still water. Plants grow thick along the edges. A single beaver family can turn a small stream into a busy wetland.\n\nSo beavers are not just builders for themselves. Their hard work helps a whole community of living things.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of this passage?","options":["Beavers have teeth that are bright orange.","Beavers build things that help them and others.","Beavers are hunted by wolves and bears."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"What makes a beaver's teeth so hard?","options":["iron","wood","mud"],"answer":0,"evidence":"They contain iron, which makes them hard and strong."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this passage, what is a lodge?","options":["a beaver baby","a beaver dam","a beaver home"],"answer":2,"evidence":"In the middle of the pond, beavers build a home called a lodge."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that ponds are good for other animals?","options":["Their enemies, such as wolves and bears, cannot easily reach them in deep water.","Ducks, frogs, fish and turtles all find homes in the still water.","The teeth never stop growing, but chewing on wood keeps them from getting too long."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Ducks, frogs, fish and turtles all find homes in the still water."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why is the lodge door hidden underwater?","options":["to keep enemies out","to let in fresh air","to catch fish for food"],"answer":0}],"grade":3,"words":219,"auto":{"fk":3.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u1-c02","unit":"G3-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Moon's Changing Shape","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Have you ever noticed that the moon seems to change shape? Some nights it looks like a bright, round ball. Other nights it is a thin curve, or you cannot see it at all. The moon is not really changing. What changes is how much of its lit side we can see.\n\nThe moon does not make its own light. Instead, sunlight shines on it, the way a flashlight shines on a ball. One half of the moon is always lit by the sun. The other half is dark.\n\nThe moon travels around Earth, and the trip takes about one month. As it moves, we see the sunny half from different angles. When the moon is between Earth and the sun, its lit side faces away from us. We call this a new moon, and the moon seems to vanish. When Earth is between the sun and the moon, we see the whole lit side. That is a full moon.\n\nBetween these times, we see parts of the lit side. A thin sliver is called a crescent. When we see half of the circle, it is called a quarter moon, because the moon has gone a quarter of the way around.\n\nThese shapes are called phases. They repeat in the same order every month. People long ago used the phases to keep track of time. In fact, the word month comes from the word moon.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is this passage mostly about?","options":["why the moon seems to change shape","how people first traveled to the moon","why the sun is brighter than the moon"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"Where does the moon's light come from?","options":["the stars","the sun","Earth"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Instead, sunlight shines on it, the way a flashlight shines on a ball."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this passage, what is a crescent?","options":["a full, round moon","a dark, empty sky","a thin sliver of moon"],"answer":2,"evidence":"A thin sliver is called a crescent."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains when we see a full moon?","options":["When Earth is between the sun and the moon, we see the whole lit side.","When the moon is between Earth and the sun, its lit side faces away from us.","Some nights it looks like a bright, round ball."],"answer":0,"evidence":"When Earth is between the sun and the moon, we see the whole lit side."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did people long ago watch the moon's phases?","options":["to know when it would rain","to tell how much time passed","to find their way home"],"answer":1}],"grade":3,"words":235,"auto":{"fk":2.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u1-c03","unit":"G3-U1","pool":"check","title":"More Than Books","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"When most people think of a library, they picture shelves full of books. Books are still the heart of every library. But today, many libraries offer much more than stories to borrow.\n\nIn some towns, you can check out things you would never expect. Some libraries lend cake pans, fishing poles and telescopes. Others lend musical instruments, such as guitars and keyboards. A few even have seed libraries. People borrow seeds, grow plants at home and bring back new seeds at the end of the season.\n\nLibraries are also places to learn new skills. Many have computers that anyone can use for free. Some have special rooms called makerspaces, with tools like sewing machines and 3D printers. Kids might build robots, make videos or learn to code.\n\nLibraries bring people together, too. They hold story times for little children and clubs for older kids. Grown-ups can take classes, practice a new language or get help finding a job. On hot summer days, a library is a cool and quiet place to rest.\n\nBest of all, most of these things are free. A library card lets everyone share the same great resources, no matter how much money they have.\n\nA modern library is like a giant toolbox for a whole town. It helps people read, learn, make and connect.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of this passage?","options":["Libraries should only lend books to readers.","Libraries offer many useful things besides books.","Libraries are too noisy for little children."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"What do people bring back to a seed library?","options":["cake pans","old books","new seeds"],"answer":2,"evidence":"People borrow seeds, grow plants at home and bring back new seeds at the end of the season."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this passage, what is a makerspace?","options":["a room with tools for making things","a shelf full of old picture books","a garden where seeds are planted"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Some have special rooms called makerspaces, with tools like sewing machines and 3D printers."},{"type":"author","q":"Why did the author write this passage?","options":["to tell how to write a book","to tell about what libraries offer","to tell why people dislike libraries"],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"The author compares a library to a toolbox. What does this mean?","options":["It is made of metal and wood.","It is filled with real hammers.","It has many useful things to share."],"answer":2}],"grade":3,"words":219,"auto":{"fk":4.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u1-c04","unit":"G3-U1","pool":"check","title":"Built for the Desert","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"A desert is a hard place to live. Days can be very hot, water is hard to find, and strong winds blow sand everywhere. Yet camels live there just fine. Their bodies are built for desert life.\n\nThe most famous part of a camel is its hump. Many people think the hump holds water, but it does not. It stores fatty tissue, which the camel's body can use for energy when food is hard to find. When a camel goes a long time without eating, its hump gets smaller and may even flop over.\n\nCamels are also great at saving water. They can go many days without a drink. When they do find water, a thirsty camel can gulp a huge amount in just a few minutes.\n\nA camel's face helps it handle sandstorms. It has two rows of long eyelashes that keep sand out of its eyes. It can also close its nostrils tight so it does not breathe in sand.\n\nEven a camel's feet are special. They are wide and padded, like big soft cushions. Instead of sinking into hot sand, the feet spread out and help the camel walk on top of it.\n\nFrom its hump to its feet, every part of a camel helps it live in one of the harshest places on Earth. People sometimes call camels the ships of the desert, because they carry people and goods across the sand.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of this passage?","options":["A camel's body helps it live in the desert.","Deserts are the hottest places on Earth.","Camels like to travel on big ships."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What does a camel's hump store?","options":["fresh water","soft sand","fatty tissue"],"answer":2,"evidence":"It stores fatty tissue, which the camel's body can use for energy when food is hard to find."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows how camels protect their eyes?","options":["It can also close its nostrils tight so it does not breathe in sand.","It has two rows of long eyelashes that keep sand out of its eyes.","They are wide and padded, like big soft cushions."],"answer":1,"evidence":"It has two rows of long eyelashes that keep sand out of its eyes."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why might a camel's hump get smaller?","options":["It has used up stored energy.","It has drunk too much water.","It has walked on hot sand."],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does harshest mean in this passage?","options":["softest and coolest","hardest to live in","most crowded"],"answer":1,"evidence":"From its hump to its feet, every part of a camel helps it live in one of the harshest places on Earth."}],"grade":3,"words":236,"auto":{"fk":4.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u2-p01","unit":"G3-U2","pool":"practice","title":"From Seed to Pumpkin","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Have you ever wondered how a big orange pumpkin grows? It all starts with one small, flat seed. The whole process takes about four months.\n\nFirst, a farmer plants pumpkin seeds in warm soil in late spring. The seeds need sunshine and plenty of water. After about a week, tiny green sprouts poke out of the ground.\n\nNext, the sprouts grow into long vines. The vines creep across the ground and can grow many feet long. Big, wide leaves grow along the vines. The leaves catch sunlight and make food for the plant.\n\nAfter that, yellow flowers bloom on the vines. Bees visit the flowers and carry pollen from one flower to another. This step is important, because a pumpkin cannot form unless a flower gets pollen.\n\nThen a small green ball appears at the bottom of a flower. This is a baby pumpkin! Over the summer, it grows bigger and bigger. Some giant pumpkins gain more than forty pounds in a single day.\n\nFinally, in early fall, the pumpkin turns bright orange. Its stem becomes hard and dry. Now it is ready to be picked, just in time for autumn festivals.\n\nWords like first, next, then and finally show the order of the steps. They help readers follow the life of a pumpkin from start to finish.","caption":"Pumpkin life cycle: seed, sprout, vine, flower, green pumpkin, orange pumpkin.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens right after the sprouts appear?","options":["They turn bright orange.","They grow into long vines.","Bees visit the flowers."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Next, the sprouts grow into long vines."},{"type":"craft","q":"How is this passage organized?","options":["in order of steps","as a problem and fix","by comparing two plants"],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why do bees matter to a pumpkin plant?","options":["They eat the plant's leaves.","They help the seeds stay warm.","They bring pollen to flowers."],"answer":2},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption show?","options":["the stages of a pumpkin's life","the best way to carve pumpkins","a list of autumn festivals"],"answer":0}],"grade":3,"words":218,"auto":{"fk":3.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u2-p02","unit":"G3-U2","pool":"practice","title":"Frogs and Toads","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Frogs and toads are close cousins. Both are amphibians, which means they start life in water and later live on land. Both lay eggs, and both hatch into tadpoles. But if you look closely, you can find many differences between them.\n\nOne difference is their skin. Most frogs have smooth, wet skin that looks shiny. Toads, on the other hand, usually have dry, bumpy skin. Some people think touching a toad gives you warts, but that is a myth.\n\nAnother difference is their legs. Frogs have long, strong back legs, so they can make big leaps. Toads have shorter legs. Instead of leaping, they usually walk or take small hops.\n\nThey also choose different homes. Most frogs stay close to water, such as ponds and streams. Toads can live farther from water, in gardens, fields and even forests.\n\nEven their eggs are different. Frogs often lay their eggs in round clumps. Toads usually lay their eggs in long strings, like a necklace of beads.\n\nSo how can you tell which is which? Check the skin and the legs. If the animal is smooth and has long legs, it is probably a frog. If it is bumpy and has short legs, it is probably a toad. Both are fun to find on a summer night!","caption":"Frog: smooth skin, long legs. Toad: bumpy skin, short legs.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What kind of skin do most toads have?","options":["smooth and wet","dry and bumpy","soft and furry"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Toads, on the other hand, usually have dry, bumpy skin."},{"type":"craft","q":"Which words show the author is comparing?","options":["first, next, then","once upon a time","on the other hand"],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"An animal has bumpy skin and short legs. What is it?","options":["probably a toad","probably a frog","probably a tadpole"],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does myth mean in this passage?","options":["an idea that is true","an idea that is false","a kind of frog song"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Some people think touching a toad gives you warts, but that is a myth."}],"grade":3,"words":214,"auto":{"fk":2.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u2-c01","unit":"G3-U2","pool":"check","title":"What Makes Thunder?","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"During a storm, a bright flash of lightning splits the sky. A few seconds later, you hear a loud boom. What causes this noise? The answer is a chain of causes and effects.\n\nLightning is a giant spark of electricity. It forms when tiny bits of ice bump into each other inside a storm cloud. The bumping builds up an electric charge. When the charge gets strong enough, a huge spark jumps through the air.\n\nA bolt of lightning is extremely hot. It can be about five times hotter than the surface of the sun! Because the bolt is so hot, it heats the air around it in a split second. The hot air spreads out very fast. This sudden push of air makes a sound wave, and that sound is thunder.\n\nYou may wonder why you see the flash before you hear the boom. Light travels much faster than sound. So the light from the lightning reaches your eyes almost at once, while the sound takes longer to reach your ears.\n\nYou can use this fact to tell how far away a storm is. Count the seconds between the flash and the thunder. Then divide by five. The answer is about how many miles away the lightning struck.\n\nIf you can hear thunder, the storm is close enough to be dangerous. Go indoors and stay there until the storm passes.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"What causes the boom of thunder?","options":["clouds that crash into each other","a sudden push of hot air","rain that hits the ground hard"],"answer":1},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens first when lightning forms?","options":["Bits of ice bump into each other.","A huge spark jumps through the air.","The hot air spreads out very fast."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It forms when tiny bits of ice bump into each other inside a storm cloud."},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the author mostly explain thunder?","options":["by telling a made-up story","by listing storms in order","by showing causes and effects"],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains why the flash comes before the boom?","options":["A bolt of lightning is extremely hot.","Light travels much faster than sound.","Then divide by five."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Light travels much faster than sound."},{"type":"inference","q":"You count ten seconds after a flash. About how far is the storm?","options":["about two miles","about ten miles","about five miles"],"answer":0}],"grade":3,"words":231,"auto":{"fk":3.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u2-c02","unit":"G3-U2","pool":"check","title":"How Paper Gets Recycled","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every day, people throw away piles of paper. Instead of filling up the trash, much of that paper can be recycled into something new. Here is how it happens, step by step.\n\nFirst, people put used paper into recycling bins. Newspapers, cardboard boxes, old homework and junk mail can all go in. A truck picks up the bins and takes the paper to a recycling center.\n\nSecond, workers and machines sort the paper. They pull out things that do not belong, such as plastic bags and pieces of metal. Different kinds of paper are put into different piles.\n\nThird, the paper is sent to a paper mill. There, it is dumped into a giant tank of water and mixed until it breaks apart. It turns into a thick, gray mush called pulp. Soap and water wash away ink and glue.\n\nNext, the clean pulp is spread out on a wide screen. The water drains through tiny holes, and the wet fibers stick together in a thin sheet. Big heated rollers press the sheet and dry it.\n\nFinally, the long sheet of new paper is wound into a huge roll. It can be cut and made into notebooks, paper towels, cereal boxes and much more.\n\nRecycling paper saves trees, water and energy. The next time you finish a page, remember that it could have a second life.","caption":"Paper recycling: collect, sort, make pulp, press and dry, roll.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens to the paper right after it is sorted?","options":["It is wound into a big roll.","It is put into a bin.","It is sent to a paper mill."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Third, the paper is sent to a paper mill."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this passage, what is pulp?","options":["a thick, gray mush","a thin, dry sheet","a pile of old mail"],"answer":0,"evidence":"It turns into a thick, gray mush called pulp."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the mill use soap and water?","options":["to make the paper gray","to wash away ink and glue","to help the paper dry"],"answer":1},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption list?","options":["the kinds of paper in bins","the trucks that carry paper","the steps of recycling paper"],"answer":2},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author use words like first, second and next?","options":["to show the order of steps","to compare two kinds of paper","to tell a funny story"],"answer":0}],"grade":3,"words":226,"auto":{"fk":4.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u2-c03","unit":"G3-U2","pool":"check","title":"Alligator or Crocodile?","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Alligators and crocodiles look a lot alike. Both are large reptiles with scaly skin, long tails and powerful jaws. Both spend much of their time in water and lay eggs on land. Still, there are some clear ways to tell them apart.\n\nThe easiest clue is the snout. An alligator has a wide, rounded snout shaped like the letter U. A crocodile's snout is longer and more pointed, shaped like the letter V.\n\nNext, look at the teeth. When an alligator closes its mouth, its upper jaw covers most of its lower teeth. When a crocodile closes its mouth, a big tooth on each side of its lower jaw still sticks out. So a crocodile looks like it has a toothy grin.\n\nThe two animals also differ in color. Alligators are usually dark, almost black. Crocodiles are often lighter, with a tan or olive green color.\n\nTheir homes are different, too. Alligators mostly live in fresh water, like swamps, lakes and rivers. Crocodiles can live in salty water near the ocean. They have special glands that remove extra salt from their bodies.\n\nAlligators live mainly in the United States and China. Crocodiles live in many warm places around the world. Southern Florida is the only place on Earth where both live side by side.\n\nWhichever you see, keep your distance. Both animals are wild and should be watched from far away.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What shape is an alligator's snout?","options":["like the letter V","like the letter U","like the letter O"],"answer":1,"evidence":"An alligator has a wide, rounded snout shaped like the letter U."},{"type":"craft","q":"How is this passage mostly organized?","options":["It compares two animals.","It tells steps in order.","It tells a made-up story."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains how crocodiles handle salty water?","options":["Alligators mostly live in fresh water, like swamps, lakes and rivers.","Crocodiles live in many warm places around the world.","They have special glands that remove extra salt from their bodies."],"answer":2,"evidence":"They have special glands that remove extra salt from their bodies."},{"type":"inference","q":"An animal's lower teeth show when its mouth is shut. What is it?","options":["an alligator","a crocodile","a lizard"],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"Where can you find both animals together?","options":["southern Florida","northern China","the open ocean"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Southern Florida is the only place on Earth where both live side by side."}],"grade":3,"words":231,"auto":{"fk":5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u2-c04","unit":"G3-U2","pool":"check","title":"The Colors of Fall Leaves","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In the fall, many trees put on a colorful show. Their green leaves turn yellow, orange and red. Then the leaves drop to the ground. Why does this happen? The answer has to do with sunlight and food.\n\nAll summer long, leaves work like little food factories. They use sunlight, water and air to make sugar, which feeds the tree. To do this job, leaves need a green material called chlorophyll. There is so much chlorophyll in a leaf that it hides the other colors.\n\nWhen fall comes, the days get shorter and cooler. There is less sunlight to make food. As a result, the tree stops making chlorophyll. The green color slowly fades away.\n\nOnce the green is gone, yellow and orange colors start to show. These colors were in the leaf the whole time. They were just hidden under the green, like a picture under a coat of paint.\n\nRed and purple leaves are a little different. Some trees, such as red maples, make these colors in the fall. Bright sunny days and cool nights help make the reds extra bright.\n\nFinally, the tree seals off each leaf where it joins the branch. The leaf dries out and falls. The tree rests through winter, and in spring it grows brand-new green leaves.","caption":"Shorter days, then less chlorophyll, then green fades, then yellow and orange show.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why does a tree stop making chlorophyll in the fall?","options":["There is too much rain.","There is less sunlight.","The tree gets too hot."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"What color is chlorophyll?","options":["green","yellow","red"],"answer":0,"evidence":"To do this job, leaves need a green material called chlorophyll."},{"type":"craft","q":"The author says leaves are like food factories. What does this mean?","options":["They are built by workers.","They sell food to animals.","They make food for the tree."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that yellow was in the leaf all along?","options":["These colors were in the leaf the whole time.","Some trees, such as red maples, make these colors in the fall.","The green color slowly fades away."],"answer":0,"evidence":"These colors were in the leaf the whole time."},{"type":"feature","q":"What does the caption show?","options":["how to plant a brand-new maple tree","the causes and effects of fall color","which trees grow the most in summer"],"answer":1}],"grade":3,"words":215,"auto":{"fk":2.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u3-p01","unit":"G3-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Priya's Big Line","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Priya had only one line in the class play, but it felt like a mountain. Every time she thought about saying it in front of the whole school, her stomach did flips.\n\n\"I'll just forget it,\" she told her grandmother at dinner. \"Everyone will stare at me.\"\n\nNani set down her spoon. \"When I was your age, I was afraid to sing in the temple choir,\" she said. \"Do you know what helped? Practice, and one friendly face.\"\n\nThat night, Priya practiced her line in the bathroom mirror. She said it to the cat, who yawned. She said it to her little brother, who giggled. She said it so many times that she began saying it in her sleep.\n\nOn the day of the play, Priya stood behind the curtain. Her hands were cold and shaky. She peeked out at the rows of chairs. Then she spotted Nani in the third row, waving a small purple scarf.\n\nWhen her turn came, Priya stepped forward. She looked right at the purple scarf. \"The king is coming, and he brings a gift for everyone!\" she called out in a loud, clear voice.\n\nThe audience clapped. Priya's face felt warm, but this time it was a good kind of warm. After the show, she ran to hug her grandmother.\n\n\"You found your friendly face,\" Nani said with a smile.\n\n\"Next year,\" Priya said, \"I want two lines.\"","questions":[{"type":"inference","q":"How does Priya feel at the start of the story?","options":["bored","nervous","angry"],"answer":1},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Priya look at the purple scarf?","options":["It is part of her new costume.","She wants to wear it on stage.","It helps her find a friendly face."],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"What did the cat do when Priya practiced?","options":["it yawned","it giggled","it waved"],"answer":0,"evidence":"She said it to the cat, who yawned."},{"type":"inference","q":"What do Priya's last words show about her?","options":["She wants to quit the play.","She feels braver now.","She is still very afraid."],"answer":1}],"grade":3,"words":234,"auto":{"fk":2.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u3-p02","unit":"G3-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Kenji's Lunch","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"On his first day at Oak Hill School, Kenji opened his lunch box slowly. Inside were three rice balls wrapped in dark green seaweed. His dad had made them that morning, just like always.\n\nKenji looked around the table. Everyone else had sandwiches. A boy named Sam was eating peanut butter and jelly. A girl named Rosa had a turkey wrap. Kenji's cheeks felt hot. He quietly closed the lid.\n\n\"Aren't you hungry?\" Rosa asked.\n\n\"Not really,\" Kenji mumbled, even though his stomach was growling.\n\nSam leaned over. \"What's in there? It smells good.\"\n\nKenji wanted to say nothing. But Sam's face looked curious, not mean. So Kenji opened the box again. \"They're called onigiri,\" he explained. \"Rice balls. My dad puts salmon in the middle.\"\n\n\"Can I try one?\" Sam asked.\n\nKenji handed him a rice ball. Sam took a big bite and chewed. Then his eyes went wide. \"This is awesome! It's way better than my sandwich.\"\n\nRosa wanted to try one too, so Kenji broke the last one in half for her. Soon the three of them were laughing and talking about their favorite foods.\n\nThat night, Kenji found his dad in the kitchen. \"Can you make extra onigiri tomorrow?\" he asked. \"I have two new friends who want some.\"\n\nHis dad smiled and reached for the rice.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why does Kenji close his lunch box at first?","options":["He is too full to eat.","He feels shy about his food.","He forgot his fork at home."],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"What kind of person is Sam?","options":["curious and friendly","rude and very bossy","quiet and very sad"],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What does Kenji's dad put inside the rice balls?","options":["turkey","peanut butter","salmon"],"answer":2,"evidence":"My dad puts salmon in the middle."},{"type":"inference","q":"How has Kenji changed by the end?","options":["He wants to eat sandwiches.","He is proud of his lunch.","He wants to sit alone."],"answer":1}],"grade":3,"words":221,"auto":{"fk":2.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u3-c01","unit":"G3-U3","pool":"check","title":"Amara and the Broken Kite","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Amara had spent all Saturday morning building a kite. She glued sticks into a cross, stretched shiny blue paper over them and tied on a long tail of rainbow ribbons. It was the best thing she had ever made.\n\nShe left it on the porch to dry and went inside for a snack. When she came back, her little brother Kofi was holding the kite. One of the sticks had snapped in two, and the paper was torn.\n\n\"Kofi!\" Amara shouted. \"You ruined it!\"\n\nKofi's lip began to shake. \"I just wanted to see it fly,\" he whispered. \"I was going to give it back.\" Big tears rolled down his cheeks.\n\nAmara was still angry. She wanted to stomp off and slam the door. But then she remembered last summer, when she had broken Dad's favorite mug by accident. She had felt terrible, and Dad had not yelled. He had helped her glue it back together.\n\nAmara took a deep breath. \"It's okay,\" she said slowly. \"Kites can be fixed. Want to help me?\"\n\nKofi wiped his nose and nodded hard.\n\nTogether they found a new stick in the garage. Amara showed Kofi how to tape the torn paper so it would hold. Kofi held the string while Amara tied knots.\n\nThat afternoon, the blue kite rose high above the park, its ribbons dancing in the wind. Kofi laughed and cheered. Amara smiled. The kite was not perfect anymore, but somehow it felt even better than before.","questions":[{"type":"inference","q":"How does Amara feel when she first sees the broken kite?","options":["proud","angry","sleepy"],"answer":1},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Amara decide to forgive Kofi?","options":["She wants Kofi to buy her a new kite.","She thinks the kite was too old anyway.","She remembers how Dad was kind to her."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Kofi feels sorry?","options":["Big tears rolled down his cheeks.","Kofi laughed and cheered.","Kofi held the string while Amara tied knots."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Big tears rolled down his cheeks."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What do Amara and Kofi do right after finding a new stick?","options":["They fly the kite at the park.","They tape the torn paper.","They eat a snack inside."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Amara showed Kofi how to tape the torn paper so it would hold."},{"type":"theme","q":"What is a lesson in this story?","options":["Never let others touch your things.","Kites are hard to build alone.","Being kind can fix more than a kite."],"answer":2}],"grade":3,"words":247,"auto":{"fk":2.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u3-c02","unit":"G3-U3","pool":"check","title":"Dev's Lemonade Stand","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Dev wanted to buy his mom a birthday present. He had his eye on a little clay pot for her plants, and it cost twelve dollars. So on Saturday, he set up a lemonade stand at the end of his driveway.\n\nHe made a bright sign that said FRESH LEMONADE, 50 CENTS. He squeezed lemons until his hands were sticky. Then he sat down and waited.\n\nAnd waited.\n\nBy noon, only two people had stopped. Dev counted his coins. He had one dollar. He felt like giving up and going inside to play video games.\n\nJust then, the mail carrier came up the street. Her name was Lena, and her face was red from the heat. Dev poured a tall cup of lemonade and held it out. \"This one is free,\" he said. \"You look really hot.\"\n\nLena drank the whole cup in one gulp. \"You just saved my day,\" she said. \"Thank you.\"\n\nDev didn't think much more about it. He stayed at his stand all afternoon, even though his legs got stiff.\n\nThe next Saturday, something surprising happened. Lena came back, and she brought four friends from the post office. Then a neighbor saw the crowd and came over too. Soon there was a line down the sidewalk!\n\nBy the end of the day, Dev had fifteen dollars. He bought the clay pot and had three dollars left over. He used the extra money to buy more lemons.\n\nWhen his mom opened her present, she hugged him tight. \"How did you earn all this?\" she asked.\n\nDev grinned. \"I had some help from a friend.\"","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why does Dev set up a lemonade stand?","options":["to get a new video game","to buy his mom a present","to help the mail carrier"],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"Which word best describes Dev when he gives Lena a cup?","options":["kind","lazy","silly"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Dev keeps trying?","options":["He felt like giving up and going inside to play video games.","Dev poured a tall cup of lemonade and held it out.","He stayed at his stand all afternoon, even though his legs got stiff."],"answer":2,"evidence":"He stayed at his stand all afternoon, even though his legs got stiff."},{"type":"literal","q":"How much money did Dev have by noon?","options":["fifteen dollars","one dollar","three dollars"],"answer":1,"evidence":"He had one dollar."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why do more people come to the stand the next Saturday?","options":["Lena tells friends about Dev.","Dev lowers his prices a lot.","Dev's mom puts up signs."],"answer":0}],"grade":3,"words":267,"auto":{"fk":1.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u3-c03","unit":"G3-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Dog Next Door","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"I used to think our neighbor's dog, Moose, was the most annoying dog on our whole street. Every afternoon, he barked and barked. He barked while I did my homework. He barked while I read my comics. He even barked while I tried to practice my violin, which made my song sound terrible.\n\n\"Why won't that dog be quiet?\" I groaned to my mom one day.\n\nMom looked out the window. \"Maybe you should go find out,\" she said.\n\nSo I did. I knocked on the door of the green house next door. Our neighbor, Grandma Vega, opened it slowly. Everyone on our street calls her that. She leaned on a cane.\n\n\"I hurt my knee last month,\" she explained. \"I can't take Moose on his long walks anymore. He gets lonely and bored, poor thing.\"\n\nI looked down at Moose. He wagged his whole body and licked my hand. Suddenly, he didn't seem so annoying. He just seemed like a dog who needed a friend.\n\n\"I could walk him,\" I said. \"If that's okay with you.\"\n\nGrandma Vega's eyes lit up.\n\nNow I walk Moose every day after school. We go all the way to the park and back. When we get home, he flops down on his rug and falls asleep. The street is quiet, and I can finally practice my violin.\n\nThe funny thing is, I kind of miss his barking on weekends. Moose isn't the most annoying dog on our street anymore. He's my best buddy.","questions":[{"type":"author","q":"Who is telling this story?","options":["Grandma Vega, who owns Moose","a kid who lives next door to Moose","Mom, who looks out the window"],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"How does the narrator feel about Moose at first?","options":["annoyed","afraid","excited"],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Moose bark so much?","options":["He hears the violin.","He wants his dinner.","He is lonely and bored."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows the narrator's feelings start to change?","options":["Suddenly, he didn't seem so annoying.","Every afternoon, he barked and barked.","He wagged his whole body and licked my hand."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Suddenly, he didn't seem so annoying."},{"type":"theme","q":"What is a lesson in this story?","options":["Dogs should always stay inside the house.","Find out why someone acts a certain way.","Only practice violin on the weekends."],"answer":1}],"grade":3,"words":250,"auto":{"fk":2.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-u3-c04","unit":"G3-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Last Word","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Marcus had studied for the school spelling bee for three whole weeks. Every night, his older sister quizzed him at the kitchen table. He could spell giraffe, mosquito and even rhinoceros without a single mistake.\n\nOn the big day, Marcus made it to the final round. Only he and a fourth grader named Lily were left on the stage. The gym was so quiet that Marcus could hear the clock ticking.\n\n\"Marcus, your word is necessary,\" said the judge.\n\nMarcus took a breath. He knew this word! But his nerves got the best of him. He spelled it with an S where the C should go.\n\nThe judge shook her head gently. \"I'm sorry, that is incorrect.\"\n\nLily stepped up and spelled it perfectly. Then she spelled one more word correctly, and the bee was over. Lily had won.\n\nMarcus felt a lump in his throat. He wanted to run off the stage and hide in the bathroom. Instead, he walked over to Lily and held out his hand.\n\n\"Great job,\" he said. \"You really earned it.\"\n\nLily smiled and shook his hand. \"You were really good too. That word is so tricky.\"\n\nOn the way home, Marcus was quiet for a while. Then he turned to his sister. \"Next year, I'm going to win,\" he said. \"And I'll never forget how to spell necessary again.\"\n\nHis sister laughed and ruffled his hair. \"I believe you,\" she said.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Who quizzed Marcus every night?","options":["his best friend","his older sister","his teacher"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Every night, his older sister quizzed him at the kitchen table."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Marcus miss the word?","options":["He gets nervous.","He never studied it.","He could not hear."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does shaking Lily's hand show about Marcus?","options":["He is happy he lost.","He wants a rematch now.","He is a good sport."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Marcus felt sad?","options":["Lily smiled and shook his hand.","Marcus felt a lump in his throat.","His sister laughed and ruffled his hair."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Marcus felt a lump in his throat."},{"type":"theme","q":"What is a lesson in this story?","options":["Be kind even when you lose.","Never enter a spelling bee.","Winning is all that matters."],"answer":0}],"grade":3,"words":238,"auto":{"fk":2.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-book-ch1","unit":"G3-U4","pool":"book","title":"Chapter 1: The Empty Cage","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Maya Okafor loved two things more than anything else: mysteries and Biscuit. Biscuit was the golden hamster who lived in Room 12. He had soft fur, round cheeks and a tiny pink nose that never stopped wiggling.\n\nEvery morning, Maya was the first student to reach the classroom. She liked to say hello to Biscuit before anyone else arrived. But on Thursday morning, something was wrong.\n\nThe cage door was wide open. The little wheel was still. Biscuit's cozy nest of paper was empty.\n\n\"Biscuit?\" Maya whispered. She looked under the cage. She checked behind the bookshelf. There was no sign of him anywhere.\n\nMiss Lopez hurried in with her coffee. When she saw the empty cage, she gasped. \"Oh no! Tomorrow is Family Day,\" she said. \"Everyone's parents are coming to meet Biscuit. What are we going to do?\"\n\nSoon the whole class had arrived. Some kids searched the coat hooks. Others peeked inside desks. A few just stood there looking worried. Ben Torres, who sat next to Maya, looked the most worried of all. He kept staring at his shoes.\n\nMaya pulled a small red notebook out of her backpack. On the cover, she had written MAYA'S CASE FILES in silver marker. She opened to a fresh page and wrote a title at the top: The Case of the Missing Hamster.\n\n\"Don't worry, Miss Lopez,\" Maya said. \"Every mystery has clues. I'm going to find Biscuit before Family Day.\"\n\nShe knelt beside the cage and looked closely. Then she spotted something on the floor. It was a tiny sunflower seed. And a little farther away, there was another one.","book":"Maya and the Missing Hamster","chapter":1,"questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What did Maya find wide open in the morning?","options":["the classroom window","the cage door","the art closet"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The cage door was wide open."},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Miss Lopez so worried about Biscuit?","options":["Families come to meet him tomorrow.","He is sick and needs a doctor.","He belongs to the principal."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Maya's notebook show about her?","options":["She likes to draw hamsters.","She forgets her homework.","She likes to solve mysteries."],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"Why might Ben keep staring at his shoes?","options":["He may have lost his new shoes.","He may know something about Biscuit.","He may be tired from running."],"answer":1}],"grade":3,"words":271,"auto":{"fk":3.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-book-ch2","unit":"G3-U4","pool":"book","title":"Chapter 2: A Trail of Seeds","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Maya followed the sunflower seeds like dots on a map. One seed lay by the reading rug. Another was near the sink. A third was right in front of the art closet door.\n\nShe wrote in her notebook: Clue 1. Seeds lead to the art closet.\n\nMaya opened the closet. Inside were shelves of paint, glue and colored paper. On the bottom shelf, she found a stack of paper that had been chewed into tiny shreds. Maya's heart jumped. Hamsters love to shred paper for their nests!\n\nShe wrote: Clue 2. Chewed paper. Biscuit was here.\n\nBut there was no hamster in the closet now. Maya searched every corner. All she found was a hole at the back, near the floor, where an old pipe went through the wall.\n\nAs she stepped out, Ben was waiting for her. He looked like he might cry.\n\n\"Maya, I have to tell you something,\" he said quietly. \"Yesterday I stayed after school to feed Biscuit. I gave him extra seeds because he looked hungry. But I promise I closed the door. I'm sure I did.\"\n\nMaya thought about it. Ben was her friend, and he was usually careful. Still, a good detective has to look at every fact.\n\n\"Will you help me check the cage?\" she asked.\n\nTogether they looked at the cage door. Maya pushed it shut, and it clicked. Then she gave it a gentle shake. The latch slid loose, and the door swung right open.\n\nBen's mouth fell open. \"It's broken!\"\n\nMaya smiled and wrote: Clue 3. The latch is loose. It wasn't Ben's fault.","book":"Maya and the Missing Hamster","chapter":2,"questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"Where was the last seed in the trail?","options":["near the classroom sink","in front of the art closet","by the reading rug"],"answer":1,"evidence":"A third was right in front of the art closet door."},{"type":"why","q":"Why was Maya excited about the chewed paper?","options":["She needed paper for art class.","Ben had left a note for her.","Hamsters shred paper for nests."],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"How does Ben feel when he talks to Maya?","options":["worried and sorry","angry and loud","silly and happy"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows why the cage door opened?","options":["I gave him extra seeds because he looked hungry.","The latch slid loose, and the door swung right open.","Ben was her friend, and he was usually careful."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The latch slid loose, and the door swung right open."}],"grade":3,"words":265,"auto":{"fk":1.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-book-ch3","unit":"G3-U4","pool":"book","title":"Chapter 3: Sounds in the Piano","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Maya looked at the hole in the back of the closet. \"Where does this pipe go?\" she asked.\n\nMiss Lopez thought for a moment. \"It goes through the wall to the music room next door,\" she said.\n\nAt lunch, Maya and Ben got permission to visit the music room. It was full of drums, music stands and an old wooden piano in the corner. The room was quiet. Too quiet.\n\nThe two friends stood very still and listened. At first they heard nothing. Then, from somewhere near the piano, came a soft sound. Scritch, scratch. Scritch, scratch.\n\n\"Did you hear that?\" Ben whispered.\n\nMaya nodded. She crept closer and pressed her ear to the side of the piano. Scritch, scratch. The sound was coming from inside!\n\nShe tried to lift the heavy lid, but it was locked. Ben tried too. It would not budge. Then the bell rang. Lunch was over, and they had to go back to class.\n\nAll afternoon, Maya could hardly sit still. What if Biscuit was stuck? What if he got hungry or cold? Family Day was tomorrow morning, and time was running out.\n\nAfter school, Maya had an idea. She asked the music teacher if they could leave a little bowl of sunflower seeds beside the piano. Then she sprinkled a thin layer of flour on the floor all around the bowl.\n\n\"What's the flour for?\" Ben asked.\n\n\"If Biscuit comes out to eat, he'll leave tiny footprints,\" Maya explained. \"Then we'll know for sure where he's hiding.\"\n\nThat night, Maya could barely sleep. She hoped her plan would work.","book":"Maya and the Missing Hamster","chapter":3,"questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where does the pipe in the closet lead?","options":["to the school office","to the playground","to the music room"],"answer":2,"evidence":"\"It goes through the wall to the music room next door,\" she said."},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author write Scritch, scratch?","options":["to show the sound they heard","to show a word Maya spelled","to show the name of a song"],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Maya put flour around the bowl?","options":["to make Biscuit a snack","to see Biscuit's footprints","to clean the dusty floor"],"answer":1},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened right after Maya and Ben tried to lift the lid?","options":["Ben heard a sound.","Maya found a seed.","The bell rang."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Then the bell rang."}],"grade":3,"words":265,"auto":{"fk":1.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g3-book-ch4","unit":"G3-U4","pool":"book","title":"Chapter 4: Case Closed","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The next morning, Maya and Ben raced to the music room. Miss Lopez and the music teacher were right behind them.\n\nThe bowl of seeds was half empty. And all across the white flour were tiny footprints! They led from the bowl to a small gap under the back of the piano.\n\n\"He's in there!\" Maya cried.\n\nThe music teacher unlocked the piano and carefully opened the back panel. Everyone held their breath. There, curled up in a nest of chewed paper and felt, was Biscuit. His cheeks were stuffed with seeds, and he blinked sleepily at them, as if to say, \"What took you so long?\"\n\nBen laughed so hard that he snorted. Miss Lopez gently scooped Biscuit into her hands. \"You found him, Maya!\" she said. \"You really found him.\"\n\nBack in Room 12, Ben helped Maya fix the cage. They wrapped a twist tie around the loose latch so it could not slide open again. Miss Lopez promised to buy a brand-new lock that afternoon.\n\nAt ten o'clock, the families arrived. Biscuit ran happily on his wheel while kids and parents crowded around. Miss Lopez told everyone the story of the great hamster escape. When she got to the part about the flour footprints, all the parents clapped for Maya and Ben.\n\nThat evening, Maya opened her red notebook one last time. Under The Case of the Missing Hamster, she wrote two words in big letters: CASE CLOSED.\n\nThen she turned to a fresh, empty page. After all, there was always another mystery waiting to be solved.","book":"Maya and the Missing Hamster","chapter":4,"questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did the footprints in the flour lead?","options":["to a hole in the closet","to a gap under the piano","to a drum in the corner"],"answer":1,"evidence":"They led from the bowl to a small gap under the back of the piano."},{"type":"inference","q":"How did the parents feel about Maya and Ben?","options":["proud of them","upset with them","bored by them"],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Maya and Ben wrap a twist tie on the latch?","options":["so the cage looks nice","so Biscuit can get out","so the door stays shut"],"answer":2},{"type":"theme","q":"What does the story's last line show about Maya?","options":["She is tired of solving cases.","She is ready for a new case.","She wants a new pet hamster."],"answer":1}],"grade":3,"words":261,"auto":{"fk":3.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-g3-01","pool":"oral-g3","title":"Busy Ants","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Ants may be tiny, but they are some of the hardest workers on Earth. An ant can carry an object many times heavier than its own body. That would be like a child lifting a small car!\n\nAnts live together in large groups called colonies. Each colony has a queen, whose job is to lay eggs. The other ants are workers. Some workers gather food. Others care for the young ants or dig new tunnels.\n\nAnts talk to each other in a special way. They leave invisible scent trails on the ground. When one ant finds food, it marks a path back home. Other ants follow the smell, one after another, until they reach the food. That is why you often see ants marching in a long, neat line.","questions":[],"grade":3,"words":129,"auto":{"fk":3.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-g3-02","pool":"oral-g3","title":"Grandpa's Pancakes","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every Sunday morning, Grandpa Joe makes pancakes. He lets me help, because he says every chef needs a good helper.\n\nFirst, I crack the eggs into a big blue bowl. Sometimes a little bit of shell falls in, and Grandpa helps me fish it out with a spoon. Then I pour in the milk and the flour while he stirs.\n\nGrandpa heats the pan until a drop of water sizzles and dances. He pours the batter into perfect circles. When tiny bubbles pop on top, it is time to flip. I hold my breath as he flips each one high into the air.\n\nOnce, a pancake landed on the floor. Our dog, Pickles, ate it in two seconds. Grandpa laughed so hard that his glasses fogged up.\n\nNow, every Sunday, we make one extra pancake just for Pickles.","questions":[],"grade":3,"words":138,"auto":{"fk":3.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-g3-03","pool":"oral-g3","title":"The Tallest Trees","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The tallest trees in the world are coast redwoods. They grow along the foggy coast of northern California. Some redwoods are taller than a thirty-story building!\n\nRedwoods can live for more than two thousand years. Their thick, reddish bark protects them from bugs and even from forest fires. In some places, the bark is about a foot thick.\n\nThese giant trees need a lot of water. In summer, there is little rain on the coast. But thick fog rolls in from the ocean almost every day. The tall trees catch water from the fog with their needles. The water drips down to the ground, where the roots can drink it.\n\nWalking through a redwood forest feels like walking through a quiet, green castle.","questions":[],"grade":3,"words":124,"auto":{"fk":3.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"oral-g3-04","pool":"oral-g3","title":"Nia's First Ride","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Nia gripped the handlebars of her shiny new bicycle while her dad held the back of the seat. \"Ready?\" he asked.\n\nNia nodded, even though her knees felt wobbly and nervous. She pushed down hard on the pedal, and the bicycle rolled slowly forward. Her dad jogged beside her, holding on tight.\n\n\"Keep pedaling!\" he called. \"Look ahead, not down at your feet!\"\n\nNia looked up at the enormous oak tree at the end of the path and pedaled faster and faster. The wind blew her braids behind her, and the bicycle felt steady and smooth.\n\n\"Dad, this is easy!\" she shouted happily.\n\nBut there was no answer. Nia glanced over her shoulder and discovered that her dad was standing far behind her, waving both arms in the air. He had let go a long time ago!\n\nNia squealed with surprise. She was riding all by herself.","questions":[],"grade":3,"words":147,"auto":{"fk":2.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}}];
